Chapter: FieldGuardMutated

 The adversarial half of field-guard-refine: a refinement on a field
 must be DROPPED when the arm can reach the record.

 A local is a value and cannot change once bound, which is why refining
 one is sound with no further condition. A field is a location: b.val is
 re-read at the store, and __record-set mutates in place. Here the guard
 proves b.val <= 15 and the arm then writes 200 into it and reads it
 back. A refinement that survived would silence this store and prove a
 200 fits 0..15 -- the lint silencing a real overflow, which is the one
 thing it must never do.

 The arm names b bare, so arm-reads-only answers False and the
 refinement is dropped. An implementation that refined a field without
 asking what the arm can reach would accept this.

Section: Definitions

  Byte = record {
   val : Integer between 0 and 255
  }

  Nibble = record {
   n : Integer between 0 and 15
  }

  mutating-arm : Byte -> Integer
  mutating-arm (b) =
   if b.val <= 15
    then let x = __record-set b "val" 200
         in (Nibble { n = b.val }).n
    else 0

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (integer-to-text (mutating-arm (Byte { val = 9 })))
  end
